<p>Myanmar&#x27;s military chief Min Aung Hlaing was elected president on Friday by parliament, formalizing his grip on state power after a recent election dominated by army-backed forces. Critics and several Western governments said the electoral sequence was designed to preserve military rule behind civilian-looking institutions, not to deliver a genuine transfer of power. He has led Myanmar since the 2021 coup that removed Aung San Suu Kyi&#x27;s elected government. Moving into the presidency keeps the same leader at the top of a country still divided by conflict, even as the office change gives military rule a formal civilian title.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/miaNDVjvV6JMbs-qIQ8OgvBeK5gOdt4m1oq3ji5ZpsXfLtoQGb0438pOxteu0HkuNQUqHMz8In2w7V5468swBF7jFPw0uAryRityqnSDKaYsUvxziqlw1jFQxLOHYm7d5Bmkibx2XmmjLZdnon1WHsp54cs8CpfEySr3fzBRipbylHsYNW6uPQYQ3TfoB4xXN3xDLmBPZSaf' alt='Myanmar’s military ruler, Min Aung Hlaing, after casting hist vote in Naypyidaw, the capital, in December. He was elected president on Friday by the country’s rubber-stamp Parliament.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Indirect route to office: The key step was a vote in the newly elected legislature, where Al Jazeera says Min Aung Hlaing cleared the majority threshold needed to become president.</li><li>Conflict trigger: The Hindu notes that the 2021 takeover put Aung San Suu Kyi under arrest, helping spark protests that later turned into nationwide armed resistance.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Western governments: They dismissed the recent election as a sham meant to preserve military rule behind a veneer of democracy. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/week-asia/politics/article/3348909/myanmars-junta-chief-min-aung-hlaing-wins-parliamentary-vote-become-president'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>New York Times framing: The paper describes Min Aung Hlaing&#x27;s elevation as the endpoint of a military-managed election sequence that shifted junta authority into a civilian 